Global warming is caused by various human activities such as burning fossil fuels for energy, deforestation, industrial processes, agriculture, and transportation. These activities release greenhouse gases like carbon dioxide (CO2), methane (CH4), nitrous oxide (N2O), and fluorinated gases into the atmosphere, which trap heat and cause the Earth's temperature to rise.
Here are some Java codes that calculate the carbon dioxide emissions from burning fossil fuels:
```
public class CarbonDioxideEmissions {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double coal = 5; // tons
        double oil = 3; // tons
        double gas = 2; // tons
        double totalEmissions = coal + oil + gas;
        System.out.println("Total CO2 emissions from burning fossil fuels: " + totalEmissions);
    }
}
```
This code calculates the total CO2 emissions from burning coal, oil, and natural gas in one year. The output will be in tons of CO2.
Deforestation also contributes to global warming because trees absorb CO2 during photosynthesis and store it in their biomass. When forests are cleared or burned, this stored carbon is released back into the atmosphere as CO2, further contributing to global warming.
